. Colour 2 occurs twice, and the output is 2.\n- **Pair 3:** the arm colours are 3, 8 (y6,x4) and 8 (y13,x10). Colour 8 occurs twice, and the output is 8.\n\n**Rule.** Find every cross: a cell of colour 4 whose four neighbours are one identical colour. Count the arm colours and output the most frequent one as a 1×1 grid.\n\n**Test.** The crosses are centred at (3,2), (8,7), (10,16), (21,4) and (24,12). Their arm colours are 2, 3, 6, 1 and 2. Colour 2 is the only repeated colour, so the answer is [[2]].\n\n**Construction.** The only stage is writing the single output cell, so there is one emit: the final 1×1 canvas.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    from collections import Counter\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    counts = Counter()\n    for y in range(1, H - 1):\n        for x in range(1, W - 1):\n            if g[y, x] != 4:\n                continue\n            nb = {int(g[y-1, x]), int(g[y+1, x]), int(g[y, x-1]), int(g[y, x+1])}\n            if len(nb) == 1:\n                counts[nb.pop()] += 1\n    colour = counts.most_common(1)[0][0] if counts else int(background(g))\n    out = np.array([[colour]], dtype=int)\n    emit(out, f\"Arm colours of the 4-centred crosses: {dict(counts)}; the most frequent arm colour {colour} is the answer.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Count the arm colours and output the most frequent one as a 1×1 grid."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Arm colours of the 4-centred crosses: {3: 2, 1: 1, 6: 1, 8: 1, 7: 1}; the most frequent arm colour 3 is the answer."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Arm colours of the 4-centred crosses: {3: 1, 2: 2, 1: 1}; the most frequent arm colour 2 is the answer."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Count the arm colours and output the most frequent one as a 1×1 grid."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"6500ea326b